# Build an Eval Suite

This notebook builds an evaluation suite for a small support agent. You define
three cases with stable IDs, score each run for task success and for correct
escalation, and print a per-case report that shows coverage and failed rows.
Then you run the same cases against a second agent configuration. The agent is
plain Elixir code with fixed replies, so the notebook runs offline with no
credentials and no model calls.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Define the agent

The agent takes a configuration and one ticket. It escalates the ticket types
listed in `config.escalate` and resolves the rest. A ticket for the
`acme-outage` account returns an error, the way a real agent run fails when a
backend times out.

```elixir
defmodule SupportAgent do
  @moduledoc "A support agent with fixed replies for offline evaluation."

  def run(_config, %{account: "acme-outage"}), do: {:error, :inventory_service_timeout}

  def run(config, %{ticket: ticket}) do
    if ticket in config.escalate do
      %{action: :escalate, reply: "Escalating to payments"}
    else
      %{action: :resolve, reply: reply(ticket)}
    end
  end

  defp reply(:password_reset), do: "Reset link sent"
  defp reply(:order_status), do: "Order shipped"
  defp reply(_ticket), do: "Ticket closed"
end
```

A runner can return a plain value, `{:ok, output}`, or `{:error, reason}`.
Tune records an error as a failed row and keeps evaluating the other cases.

## Write the cases

Give every case an `id`. Tune uses it to name observations, and paired
comparisons use it to match rows. Keep IDs stable so reports from different
runs line up.

```elixir
cases = [
  %{
    id: "password-reset",
    input: %{ticket: :password_reset, account: "north-bakery"},
    expected: %{action: :resolve, reply: "Reset link sent"}
  },
  %{
    id: "chargeback",
    input: %{ticket: :chargeback, account: "lumen-books"},
    expected: %{action: :escalate, reply: "Escalating to payments"}
  },
  %{
    id: "order-status",
    input: %{ticket: :order_status, account: "acme-outage"},
    expected: %{action: :resolve, reply: "Order shipped"}
  }
]
```

The first case is a routine request. The second needs an escalation. The third
fails because the agent run returns an error.

## Build the suite

`Tune.new/2` takes the configuration to evaluate and a `run:` function.
`Tune.data/2` adds the cases as the `test` split. Each `Tune.score/4` call adds
one named metric. A two-argument callback receives the case and the output.

```elixir
task_success = fn tune_case, output ->
  if output.reply == tune_case.expected.reply, do: 1, else: 0
end

correct_escalation = fn tune_case, output ->
  if output.action == tune_case.expected.action, do: 1, else: 0
end

suite = fn config ->
  Tune.new(config, run: &SupportAgent.run/2)
  |> Tune.data(test: cases)
  |> Tune.score(:task_success, task_success)
  |> Tune.score(:correct_escalation, correct_escalation)
end
```

The builder calls only return a `%Tune{}` value. Nothing runs until you call
`Tune.evaluate/2`.

## Run the evaluation

```elixir
{:ok, evaluation} = Tune.evaluate(suite.(%{escalate: [:chargeback]}))

{evaluation.complete?, length(evaluation.observations)}
```

```text
{true, 3}
```

`Tune.evaluate/2` returns a `Tune.Evaluation`. It holds one `Tune.Observation`
per case, in dataset order, and one `Tune.Score.Summary` per score.
`complete?` is `true` because Tune attempted every case, even though one case
failed.

## Print a per-case report

Write the report as an ordinary function so you can reuse it for every
configuration.

```elixir
defmodule SuiteReport do
  @moduledoc "Prints observations, summaries, and failed rows for one evaluation."

  def print(evaluation, metrics) do
    Enum.each(evaluation.observations, &print_row(&1, metrics))

    Enum.each(metrics, fn name ->
      summary = Tune.Evaluation.summary(evaluation, name)

      IO.puts(
        "#{name}: mean #{summary.mean}, scored #{summary.count} of " <>
          "#{length(evaluation.observations)}, coverage #{Float.round(summary.coverage, 2)}"
      )
    end)

    failed = for %{status: :error} = row <- evaluation.observations, do: {row.id, row.error}
    IO.puts("failed rows: #{inspect(failed)}")
  end

  defp print_row(row, metrics) do
    values = Enum.map_join(metrics, " ", &"#{&1}=#{Map.get(row.scores, &1, "-")}")
    IO.puts(String.pad_trailing(row.id, 16) <> String.pad_trailing("#{row.status}", 7) <> values)
  end
end

SuiteReport.print(evaluation, [:task_success, :correct_escalation])
```

```text
password-reset  ok     task_success=1 correct_escalation=1
chargeback      ok     task_success=1 correct_escalation=1
order-status    error  task_success=- correct_escalation=-
task_success: mean 1.0, scored 2 of 3, coverage 0.67
correct_escalation: mean 1.0, scored 2 of 3, coverage 0.67
failed rows: [{"order-status", :inventory_service_timeout}]
```

Read the mean together with the coverage. Both metrics have a mean of `1.0`,
but they cover only two of the three cases. The failed row has no scores, and
its `error` field keeps the reason the runner returned.

## Test a second configuration

Run the same suite on a configuration that escalates password resets too. The
cases and scores stay the same, so the two reports compare row by row.

```elixir
{:ok, eager} = Tune.evaluate(suite.(%{escalate: [:chargeback, :password_reset]}))

SuiteReport.print(eager, [:task_success, :correct_escalation])
```

```text
password-reset  ok     task_success=0 correct_escalation=0
chargeback      ok     task_success=1 correct_escalation=1
order-status    error  task_success=- correct_escalation=-
task_success: mean 0.5, scored 2 of 3, coverage 0.67
correct_escalation: mean 0.5, scored 2 of 3, coverage 0.67
failed rows: [{"order-status", :inventory_service_timeout}]
```

The `password-reset` row shows where the second configuration loses: it
escalates a routine request. Use `Tune.compare/3` when you need the per-row
changes and paired coverage as data instead of two printed reports.

